"""AIONS Skill Engine - semantic (Chroma/CBMS) retrieval over skills.

WHAT THIS FILE DOES
--------------------
Bridges control_plane/skills/registry.py (lexical, stem-based token overlap)
to the SAME always-on Chroma HTTP store CBMS already uses
(server/store_selector.VectorStore), following the pattern
control_plane/skills/recipes.py already established for recipe intents
(session_aions_recipes). Creates/queries ONE Chroma collection:
session_aions_skills (via VectorStore._get_coll("aions_skills")). Reuses the
DEFAULT embedding function (all-MiniLM-L6-v2, 384 dims, same as every other
collection in this Chroma instance) -- no extra imports, no model mismatch.

v2 (evidence-gated rebuild, 2026-07-31): v1 of this module was never wired
into any caller. Head-to-head on a 48-case labelled test set
(runtime/skill_retrieval_testset.json; 17 PL incl. diacritics, 26
natural-speech/paraphrase, 5 no-capability "nothing" cases) built from the
real skills_lib/ corpus found:
  - registry.search() alone: top1 51.2% (22/43), top3 62.8%, but 80% (4/5)
    FALSE-POSITIVE rate on "nothing" queries.
  - raw Chroma similarity alone: top1 55.8% (24/43), top3 76.7%, 0/5 FP at a
    0.35 floor -- but a real, reproducible weakness: full-sentence Polish
    queries pull in a cluster of short-blob skills (paper.done, note.save,
    skill.request, web.search) as embedding "attractors" that outscore the
    correct answer, sometimes keeping it out of the top 10 entirely. This is
    an embedding-quality gap, not a threshold artifact -- the WRONG
    candidate has the HIGHEST raw score, so no threshold fixes it.
  - v1's merge (semantic hits unconditionally first if >=0.35, then lexical
    top-up) actively made things worse than lexical alone on several PL
    queries: no way to prefer a correct lexical winner over an
    above-threshold-but-wrong semantic attractor.
This version replaces that merge with a calibrated blend re-ranker (see
semantic_skill_search() below), tuned on split A of the test set and
verified COLD on the held-out split B:
    held-out top1: hybrid 61.9% (13/21) vs lexical 42.9% (9/21)
    held-out top3: hybrid 71.4% (15/21) vs lexical 57.1% (12/21)
    held-out FP :  hybrid 50.0% (1/2)   vs lexical 100.0% (2/2)
4 cases fixed, 0 regressions on the held-out half.

v3 (iterative optimisation task, R0-R4, 2026-07-31): test set expanded
48->92 cases, tune/holdout split re-randomised ONCE (fixed seed, see
runtime/skill_retrieval_split.json), never touched again. Held-out results
per round: R0 baseline top1 56.4%/top3 64.1%/FP 71.4%. R1 multi-doc index
(session_aions_skills_v2, MAX-over-docs) top1 56.4%/top3 66.7%/FP 71.4% --
kept (no regression). R2 anti-hub score penalty: top1 56.4% flat, FP
WORSENED to 85.7% in isolation -- honest negative, superseded by R3. R3
joint grid retune (2000+ points, tune-half only, fp-tiered selection after
a naive top1-only search overfit the 3-case tune none-sample to 100%
held-out FP): winning config has hub_w=0 -- plain retuned thresholds beat
every hub-corrected variant. FINAL top1 59.0%/top3 66.7%/FP 57.1%. R4
per-skill dynamic sem-only bar: tied R3 exactly (0pp) -- confirmed plateau,
reverted to the simpler flat-threshold R3 config. Ceiling: held-out top1
did not move past ~59% under any blend/threshold/anti-hub combination
tried; remaining misses are hard paraphrases (embedding-similarity ceiling)
and sibling-skill families (app.*, window.*, linux.service.*/files.*/
logs.*) that are genuinely close in embedding space -- fixing these needs a
different/stronger embedder, out of scope here by design.

WHY IT EXISTS
-------------
SkillRegistry.search() scores purely on stemmed token overlap. A query that
paraphrases a skill without sharing a surface token (e.g. PL "nagly wysyp
awarii" vs. tags "errors"/"spike"/"count") scores exactly 0 and is
structurally invisible to any caller, including goal_planner.plan_goal().
This module adds semantic retrieval BLENDED with (never simply replacing)
the lexical path.

PUBLIC API
----------
build_skill_index(registry=None, force=False) -> dict
    One-time / re-runnable full build of session_aions_skills from the
    current skills_lib/. Unchanged from v1. upsert() is keyed by skill id.

semantic_skill_search(query, top_k=8, threshold=None, registry=None) -> list[dict]
    HYBRID re-ranker over the UNION of lexical (registry.search) and
    semantic (Chroma) candidates. Return shape IDENTICAL to
    SkillRegistry.search(): [{"id": str, "type": "skill", "score": float}].

    Scoring: final = W_LEX * normalised_lexical + W_SEM * semantic_similarity
      - normalised_lexical = min(1.0, raw_lexical_score / LEX_SATURATION) --
        a FIXED saturation point, NOT per-query max-normalisation (tried,
        measurably worse: lets one incidental token overlap on an unrelated
        "nothing" query rescale to 1.0 just because nothing else scored
        higher in that query either).
      - semantic_similarity is Chroma's native 0..1 cosine similarity.
    Acceptance:
      - found by BOTH, or by lexical alone: needs final >= BASE_MIN.
      - found by semantic ALONE (lexical score exactly 0): needs its RAW
        semantic_similarity >= SEM_ONLY_MIN, a materially HIGHER standalone
        bar -- keeps PL "attractor" skills from winning on semantic alone.
    threshold= (legacy kwarg) overrides SEM_ONLY_MIN for this call only.

ENV VARS
--------
    AIONS_SKILL_SEMANTIC=1|0              (default 1) master on/off switch.
                                           "0": returns EXACTLY
                                           registry.search()'s output.
    AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_W_LEX=0.4          weight on normalised lexical
                                           score (w_sem is always 1-w_lex).
    AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_LEX_SATURATION=6.0 raw lexical score treated as
                                           "fully confident" (-> norm 1.0).
    AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_BASE_MIN=0.25      min blended score to be returned.
    AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_SEM_ONLY_MIN=0.4   min RAW semantic similarity for a
                                           semantic-only candidate.
All four tuned defaults come from a joint grid search (2000+ points) on the
tune half of the 92-case test set (fixed split in
runtime/skill_retrieval_split.json), selected via fp-count-tiering to avoid
overfitting the thin none-case sample, frozen and verified cold on the
held-out half -- see v3 note above.

SAFETY / FAILURE MODE
----------------------
Every Chroma/HTTP call here is wrapped in try/except. If Chroma is down, the
collection doesn't exist yet, or the response shape is wrong,
semantic_skill_search() DEGRADES SILENTLY to exactly registry.search(query,
top_k=top_k, types=("skill",))'s output -- same spirit as
control_plane/cbms_gate.py::_chroma_hits() and
control_plane/skills/recipes.py::find_recipe_semantic(). Never raises into a
caller from a Chroma-side failure; never writes/deletes/modifies any Chroma
collection other than session_aions_skills.

NOTE (behaviour change from v1): when Chroma IS available but the blend
legitimately finds no candidate clearing its bar (most often a genuine
"nothing" query), this now returns [] instead of falling back to raw
lexical hits. v1's "always return at least what lexical would" guarantee is
exactly what capped lexical's false-positive rate as a ceiling nobody could
improve on -- beating that ceiling was the point of this rebuild. Callers
already treat an empty list as "no capability found" (goal_planner.plan_goal:
"if not candidates: return escalate").

Anti-hallucination: any semantic hit whose id is not currently present in
the loaded SkillRegistry (registry.get(id) is None) is dropped before it can
reach a caller -- protects against a stale vector for a skill deleted or
renamed in skills_lib/ since the last build_skill_index() run.

ROLLBACK
--------
1. Fastest (no file changes): set env var AIONS_SKILL_SEMANTIC=0.
   semantic_skill_search() then returns exactly registry.search()'s output.
2. If wired into a caller (e.g. goal_planner.py), revert that one-line
   `from control_plane.skills.semantic_search import semantic_skill_search`
   / call-site change back to `registry.search(...)`.
3. Full removal: delete this file, optionally delete the Chroma
   collection(s):
       DELETE http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/collections/session_aions_skills
       DELETE http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/collections/session_aions_skills_v2
   Zero effect on any other collection -- this module only ever touches
   "aions_skills" (legacy v1, via the legacy build_skill_index() below) and
   "aions_skills_v2" (active, via runtime/build_skill_index_v2.py).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any

REPO = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
if str(REPO) not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO))

from . import registry as registry_mod  # noqa: E402

_PL_DIACRITICS_MAP = str.maketrans({
    "ą": "a", "ć": "c", "ę": "e", "ł": "l", "ń": "n",
    "ó": "o", "ś": "s", "ź": "z", "ż": "z",
    "Ą": "a", "Ć": "c", "Ę": "e", "Ł": "l", "Ń": "n",
    "Ó": "o", "Ś": "s", "Ź": "z", "Ż": "z",
})


def _normalize_text(s: str) -> str:
    """Lowercase + fold PL diacritics to base Latin letters. Mirrors
    runtime/build_skill_index_v2.py's doc-side normalize_text EXACTLY --
    queries and documents must be folded identically before embedding."""
    if not s:
        return s
    return s.lower().translate(_PL_DIACRITICS_MAP)


_SKILL_SESSION_V1 = "aions_skills"     # legacy single-blob-per-skill (rollback target, untouched by v3)
_SKILL_SESSION_V2 = "aions_skills_v2"  # multi-document-per-skill (see runtime/build_skill_index_v2.py)
_SKILL_SESSION = _SKILL_SESSION_V2      # ACTIVE collection as of v3 -- v2 won (module docstring v3 note)

# v3 (iterative optimisation, 2026-07-31): re-tuned on the expanded 92-case
# test set, tune/holdout split fixed once (seed 20260731).
_W_LEX_DEFAULT = 0.4
_LEX_SATURATION_DEFAULT = 6.0
_BASE_MIN_DEFAULT = 0.25
_SEM_ONLY_MIN_DEFAULT = 0.4

_POOL_K_LEXICAL = 30    # >> corpus size headroom (87 skills) so "found by
                        # lexical at all" is never an artifact of a small pool
_POOL_K_SEMANTIC = 100  # v2 indexes ~6-10 raw DOCUMENTS per skill, so the
                        # raw candidate pool must be wide enough to surface
                        # enough DISTINCT skills after grouping by skill_id
                        # -- 100 is Chroma's own per-query cap.

_VS: Any = None          # in-process VectorStore singleton
_REGISTRY: Any = None    # in-process SkillRegistry singleton


def semantic_enabled() -> bool:
    return os.environ.get("AIONS_SKILL_SEMANTIC", "1").lower() in ("1", "true", "on", "yes")


def _env_float(name: str, default: float) -> float:
    try:
        return float(os.environ.get(name, default))
    except Exception:
        return default


def _weight_lex() -> float:
    w = _env_float("AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_W_LEX", _W_LEX_DEFAULT)
    return min(1.0, max(0.0, w))


def _lex_saturation() -> float:
    v = _env_float("AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_LEX_SATURATION", _LEX_SATURATION_DEFAULT)
    return v if v > 0 else _LEX_SATURATION_DEFAULT


def _base_min() -> float:
    return _env_float("AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_BASE_MIN", _BASE_MIN_DEFAULT)


def _sem_only_min() -> float:
    return _env_float("AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_SEM_ONLY_MIN", _SEM_ONLY_MIN_DEFAULT)


def _get_vs():
    """Lazily create VectorStore (HTTP -> always-on Chroma :8000, embedded
    fallback). Singleton per process. Returns None if unavailable -- callers
    MUST treat that as "fall back to lexical", never as a hard error."""
    global _VS
    if _VS is not None:
        return _VS
    try:
        os.environ.setdefault("CHROMA_USE_HTTP", "true")
        from server.store_selector import VectorStore  # type: ignore

        _VS = VectorStore()
        return _VS
    except Exception:
        return None


def _get_registry():
    """Lazily create + discover a SkillRegistry singleton, reused both as the
    source of truth for build_skill_index() and as the lexical side +
    anti-hallucination check inside semantic_skill_search()."""
    global _REGISTRY
    if _REGISTRY is not None:
        return _REGISTRY
    reg = registry_mod.SkillRegistry()
    reg.discover()
    _REGISTRY = reg
    return _REGISTRY


def build_skill_index(registry=None, force: bool = False) -> dict:
    """LEGACY (v1, single-blob-per-skill) builder -- kept only as a
    rollback target. The ACTIVE collection is session_aions_skills_v2,
    built/rebuilt via runtime/build_skill_index_v2.py; this function always
    targets _SKILL_SESSION_V1 explicitly (never the swappable _SKILL_SESSION)
    so it can never accidentally corrupt v2's multi-document structure.

    One-time / re-runnable full build of the session_aions_skills Chroma
    collection from the current skills_lib/ contents.

    Safe to call repeatedly (e.g. after forge.py writes a new skill.json, or
    from a periodic task): upsert() is keyed by the stable skill id, so
    re-running after an edit updates the existing vector in place instead of
    erroring on a duplicate id, and a brand-new skill just gets added.

    `force` is accepted for API symmetry with other AIONS build/rebuild
    entrypoints; because upsert is always idempotent here there is currently
    no cheaper "skip" path -- kept as a parameter so a future version can add
    a change-hash short-circuit without breaking callers.

    Returns a dict describing the outcome; never raises.
    """
    try:
        reg = registry or _get_registry()
        if registry is None:
            reg.discover()  # re-read skills_lib/ from disk so edits/new skills are picked up

        vs = _get_vs()
        if vs is None:
            return {"ok": False, "reason": "vector_store_unavailable", "indexed": 0}

        try:
            coll = vs._get_coll(_SKILL_SESSION_V1)  # legacy v1 target ONLY
        except Exception as e:
            return {"ok": False, "reason": f"collection_unavailable: {e}", "indexed": 0}

        ids, docs, metas = [], [], []
        for obj in reg.objects.values():
            if obj.type != "skill":
                continue
            # Reuse AObject.blob() (id + name + description + tags) -- the SAME
            # text the lexical registry.search() already scores against, so the
            # two retrieval paths stay conceptually aligned.
            ids.append(obj.id)
            docs.append(obj.blob())
            metas.append({
                "name": obj.name,
                "description": obj.description,
                "tags": " ".join(obj.tags),
                "risk": obj.risk,
                "handler": obj.handler,
                "source_dir": obj.source_dir,
                "type": "skill",
            })

        if not ids:
            return {"ok": True, "indexed": 0, "reason": "no_skills_found"}

        try:
            coll.upsert(ids=ids, documents=docs, metadatas=metas)
        except Exception as e:
            return {"ok": False, "reason": f"upsert_failed: {e}", "indexed": 0}

        return {"ok": True, "indexed": len(ids), "collection": f"session_{_SKILL_SESSION_V1}"}
    except Exception as e:
        return {"ok": False, "reason": f"unexpected_error: {e}", "indexed": 0}


def semantic_skill_search(query: str, top_k: int = 8, threshold: float | None = None,
                           registry=None) -> list:
    """Hybrid (lexical UNION semantic) re-ranker. See module docstring for the
    scoring formula, acceptance rule, and the held-out evidence behind the
    tuned defaults.

    If AIONS_SKILL_SEMANTIC=0, returns EXACTLY registry.search(query,
    top_k=top_k, types=("skill",))'s output -- nothing more, nothing less.

    `threshold`, if given, overrides AIONS_SKILL_HYBRID_SEM_ONLY_MIN for this
    call only (legacy kwarg name kept for signature compatibility).
    """
    reg = registry or _get_registry()

    # Lexical side is always computed first: it is both a scoring input AND
    # the mandatory silent-degrade path if semantic turns out unavailable.
    lexical_hits = reg.search(query, top_k=_POOL_K_LEXICAL, types=("skill",))

    if not semantic_enabled():
        return lexical_hits[:top_k]

    lex_scores = {h["id"]: h["score"] for h in lexical_hits}

    semantic_scores: dict = {}
    semantic_available = False
    try:
        vs = _get_vs()
        if vs is not None:
            nq = _normalize_text(query)
            raw = vs.search(_SKILL_SESSION, nq, top_k=_POOL_K_SEMANTIC)
            semantic_available = True
            for h in raw or []:
                # v2 indexes SEVERAL documents per skill sharing metadata
                # {"skill_id": ...}; score per skill = MAX over its docs.
                meta = h.get("metadata") or {}
                sid = meta.get("skill_id")
                if not sid or reg.get(sid) is None:
                    # Anti-hallucination: id not currently loaded in registry
                    # (stale vector for a deleted/renamed skill) -- drop it.
                    continue
                sc = float(h.get("score") or 0.0)
                if sid not in semantic_scores or sc > semantic_scores[sid]:
                    semantic_scores[sid] = sc
    except Exception:
        semantic_available = False

    if not semantic_available:
        # Chroma down / collection missing / bad response -> silent degrade
        # to pure lexical, per module contract.
        return lexical_hits[:top_k]

    w_lex = _weight_lex()
    w_sem = 1.0 - w_lex
    lex_sat = _lex_saturation()
    base_min = _base_min()
    sem_only_min = float(threshold) if threshold is not None else _sem_only_min()

    candidate_ids = set(lex_scores) | set(semantic_scores)
    scored = []
    for cid in candidate_ids:
        lraw = lex_scores.get(cid, 0.0)
        sraw = semantic_scores.get(cid, 0.0)
        lnorm = min(1.0, lraw / lex_sat) if lex_sat > 0 else 0.0
        final = w_lex * lnorm + w_sem * sraw

        found_by_lexical = cid in lex_scores
        if not found_by_lexical and sraw < sem_only_min:
            # semantic-only candidate below the higher standalone bar -> drop
            continue
        if final < base_min:
            continue

        if reg.get(cid) is None:
            continue  # anti-hallucination (belt-and-braces)

        scored.append((final, cid))

    scored.sort(key=lambda t: (-t[0], t[1]))
    return [{"id": cid, "type": "skill", "score": round(final, 4)} for final, cid in scored[:top_k]]
